ntries_b64` against the predicate.\n\nI think the monitor command is the right place because that's where the predicate from the Rekor attestation is available. During ingest/verify, we don't have the predicate to check against (the predicate is in the `statement_b64` which is the signed statement from the writer).\n\nActually, wait - the predicate is inside the `statement_b64` which is part of the orphan entry itself. So we could potentially validate it during `load_chain` or `cmd_verify` by decoding the `statement_b64` and extracting the predicate. But the task specifically mentions the monitor command context: \"A reviewer found that an orphan explained only by its statement could hide an attested entry, such as a failing verdict.\"\n\nThe monitor command checks orphans against Rekor, so that's where the validation makes the most sense. The predicate comes from the Rekor entry (via the statement), and we check that the orphan's `entries_b64` matches that predicate.\n\nLet me also verify the function works correctly by testing it manually.",
